  "A": {
   "verdict": "NOT_CODE",
   "failure_scenario": "The reviewer identifies an unspecified design choice that could cause stalls in a future implementation; this documentation change introduces no runtime behavior.",
   "reproduction": {
    "command": "git diff --stat c06bce4946f2f9ccb37670638966dc4c8b292e7f..c48aa359b1cee60dfeca6ed1958560926299e27a",
    "output": " docs/ARCHITECTURE.md       |   3 +\n docs/privacy-boundaries.md | 308 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++\n 2 files changed, 311 insertions(+)"
   },
   "evidence": "HEAD explicitly says, 'implementation belongs to #919 (c2)' and 'No performance measurement was made for this documentation change.' Lines 204\u2013208 label the CPU figures 'planning estimates only'; they neither implement synchronous checks nor require daemon-thread execution. The document is absent at base, and executable code is unchanged. The original request requires cost estimates and an affordable mechanism, but does not explicitly require an execution-thread choice, stall ceiling, fresh measurements, or future decoding support. F1 requests additional design and benchmark acceptance requirements rather than demonstrating a code defect at HEAD."
  },
  "B": {
   "verdict": "REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "Someone building #919 from this design document gets a plan for one to three full private-string checks of 5 MiB per gate, estimated at 1.3 to 3.9 seconds of CPU, but no guidance on where that work runs and no limit on how long the daemon may block. A straightforward build would run the checks on the daemon's single thread and freeze it for seconds on every large gate. Nothing breaks at this head, because the change is documentation only.",
   "reproduction": {
    "command": "grep -n -i -E 'stall|worker thread|event loop|event-loop|yield|synchron' docs/privacy-boundaries.md; git log --oneline c06bce49 | grep '#921'",
    "output": "No grep match mentions an event-loop stall, a worker thread, yields or synchronous slices. The only 'worker' hits are about WorkerStore and remote workers, which are boundaries, not where checks run. The git log shows 'cac652b5 Private matcher: replace the trailing-escape regex with a linear scan (ReDoS) (#921)' is already in base."
   },
   "evidence": "Head docs/privacy-boundaries.md:183-215 says: \"No performance measurement was made for this documentation change. The issue reports about 1.3 s for privateMatches on 5 MiB with eight private entries\" and \"Estimate for unchanged decoder cost: ~1.3 s private work with one form source, ~2.6 s with raw plus different visible source, and up to ~3.9 s with another joined source\". The section budgets CPU but never says whether that work runs on the daemon thread, in a worker, or in yielded slices, and sets no stall budget. Its c2 slice table (around line 300) has no stall acceptance criterion either. The issue asks for the design to say how streams keep the check affordable, and multi-second synchronous work on the daemon thread is not affordable. The baseline sub-point is partly valid: #921 is in base, so the 1.3 s figure predates it, but the document labels 1.3 s as the issue's unmeasured figure. #923 had not landed, so citing it is advisory, not a defect."
  }
 }
]
